; SelfMind macOS exec sandbox — base policy.
;
; Mirrors the bubblewrap baseline semantically, not mechanically: a read-only
; view of the host, writes confined to declared roots, and no egress unless the
; policy shares the network. Seatbelt is a permission filter, so the namespace
; isolation bwrap provides (PID/IPC/UTS, mount) has no equivalent here; see
; docs/execution-engine.zh-CN.md for the recorded differences.
;
; Written against the documented SBPL primitives. The set of operations a shell
; and ordinary developer toolchains actually need was cross-checked against
; codex's seatbelt profile (codex-rs/sandboxing/src/seatbelt_base_policy.sbpl),
; which credits Chromium's macOS sandbox policy for the same ground.
;
; The generated suffix appends the writable-root and network clauses; this file
; must stay valid on its own so a profile with no writable roots is still a
; closed sandbox rather than a parse error.

(version 1)

; Closed by default: every operation below is an explicit, reviewed opening.
(deny default)

; Children inherit this policy, so a shell may fork and exec normally.
(allow process-exec)
(allow process-fork)
(allow signal (target same-sandbox))
(allow process-info* (target same-sandbox))

; Read-only view of the host. This is the direct analogue of bwrap's
; `--ro-bind / /`: reads are unrestricted, writes are not. Credential exposure
; is governed separately by the approval layer's credential axis, exactly as it
; is under bubblewrap.
(allow file-read*)

; Unrestricted sysctl reads. Restricting them buys only fingerprinting
; resistance, which is not this sandbox's threat model, and a partial list is a
; long tail of mysterious toolchain failures. bwrap likewise leaves host
; sysctls readable.
(allow sysctl-read)

; Some CPU feature probes are classified as writes because userspace hands the
; kernel a buffer; conceptually they are reads.
(allow sysctl-write (sysctl-name "kern.grade_cputype"))

; Discarding output must work even with no writable root.
(allow file-write-data
  (require-all
    (path "/dev/null")
    (vnode-type CHARACTER-DEVICE)))

; Interactive tools check for a tty. Without this an ordinary shell reports no
; terminal and changes its behaviour.
(allow pseudo-tty)
(allow file-read* file-write* file-ioctl (literal "/dev/ptmx"))
(allow file-read* file-write*
  (require-all
    (regex #"^/dev/ttys[0-9]+")
    (extension "com.apple.sandbox.pty")))
; A pty created before the sandbox was entered carries no extension, so allow
; ioctl on the slave directly.
(allow file-ioctl (regex #"^/dev/ttys[0-9]+"))

; mach-lookup stays an explicit allowlist. A blanket allow would let a confined
; process reach arbitrary system services, which is a real escape surface on
; macOS. Only user/group resolution is needed with the network disabled.
(allow mach-lookup
  (global-name "com.apple.system.opendirectoryd.libinfo"))

; POSIX semaphores: Python's multiprocessing needs them to start at all.
(allow ipc-posix-sem)
